# Workflow

Every change to code must end with all three steps done. A change that is built but not restarted is not testable; a change that is restarted but not pushed is not saved.

## 1. Rebuild

```bash
./build.sh
```

Compiles all of `Sources/**/*.swift` (no package manifest — new files need no registration), bundles, codesigns, and installs to `~/Applications/Better Mac Taskbar.app`. Any `error:` in the output is blocking.

## 2. Restart the running app

```bash
pkill -x BetterMacTaskbar; sleep 1; open ~/Applications/"Better Mac Taskbar.app"
```

`open` on its own just focuses the already-running process and the new binary never loads. The old process must die first.

## 3. Commit and push to the default branch

```bash
git add -A && git commit -m "..." && git push origin main
```

The default branch is **`main`** (there is no `master`). Do not leave finished work uncommitted, and do not create a side branch for ordinary changes.

## Notes

- Claude Code runs steps 1 and 2 automatically via a `Stop` hook (`.claude/hooks/rebuild-restart.sh`) when sources are newer than the built binary. It never commits or pushes — step 3 is always explicit.
- SourceKit shows `Cannot find type 'WindowInfo' in scope` and similar across most files because there is no package manifest defining the module. **These are false positives.** `./build.sh` is the only authority on whether the code compiles.
- Verify user-visible changes against the running app, not just by reading the code. For the window layer use `./scripts/bmt-log snap` (see `app-logs.mdc`).
